[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because the report would otherwise be empty, the section below records the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviours that *had to be matched* — each one is a place where a naive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation would have diverged, and each has a dedicated test. These were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m verified as correct in the existing Rust source rather than fixed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `scanf` skips newlines; `fgets` would not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d", &data[i])` skips **any** leading whitespace — space, `\t`, `\n`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `\v`, `\f`, `\r` — before looking for a digit. So `"1 2 3"`, `"1\n2\n3"` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"1\r\n2\x0b3"` are all the same input, and whitespace-only input is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m indistinguishable from EOF.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A line-oriented reader (`fgets`/`BufRead::lines` + one parse per line) would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m diverge on the very first multi-value-per-line input. `Scanner::scan_i32`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m skips whitespace bytes directly instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `whitespace_only_is_eof`, `scanf_reads_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed with the mutation `is_space(c) && c != b'\n'` (i.e. line-oriented
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reading): the suite fails on `"4\n-5\n6\n0\n7"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `%d` converts via `long`, saturating, then truncates to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc implements `%d` on LP64 by running a `strtol`-equivalent and assigning the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `long` result to an `int`. On overflow `strtol` **clamps** to `LONG_MIN` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MAX` (setting `ERANGE`) and the assignment then **truncates** the low 32
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bits. The two steps do not commute:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | `long` after clamp | stored `int` | printed `x*x+x` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` | `2147483648` | `-2147483648` | `-2147483648` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` | `4294967296` | `0` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `99999999999999999999999999` | `LONG_MAX` (clamped) | `-1` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-99999999999999999999999999` | `LONG_MIN` (clamped) | `0` | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note the asymmetry in the last two rows: a hugely positive literal yields `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and a hugely negative one yields `0`. Reducing an arbitrary-precision value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m modulo 2³² — the obvious translation — gives the wrong answer for anything past
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the `long` range, and `i64::from_str` returning `Err` on overflow would give
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the wrong answer too. `saturate_then_truncate` performs clamp-then-truncate in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `out_of_int_range_values`, `absurdly_long_digit_runs`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `full_array_of_extremes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed with the mutation that drops the `LONG_MIN` clamp: the suite fails on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"-99999999999999999999999999"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Related detail: digit accumulation is guarded by `OVERFLOW_GUARD` so a 5,000-digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run cannot overflow the accumulator itself. The guard stops accumulating once the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m magnitude already exceeds `i64::MAX`, which is safe precisely because the result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is clamped anyway.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Signed overflow in `out[i] * out[i] + out[i]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fma_array` is called as `fma_array(out, out, out, out, len)` — all four
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pointers alias the same buffer — so each element becomes `x*x + x` computed from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m its own current value. Signed overflow is UB in C; the compiled code uses `imul`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and wraps, at `-O0` and `-O3` alike. The Rust uses `wrapping_mul` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_add`, so e.g. `46341` prints `-2147383295` rather than panicking (a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m debug build with plain `*` would abort) or saturating.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `signed_overflow_in_the_fma`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. The `i < 100` bound, and what happens at exactly 100
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` reads into `int data[100]` and the loop has two exits: the count reaching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100, or `scanf` returning anything other than 1. Reaching 100 exits **without
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m consuming another token**, so trailing garbage after the 100th value is never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m examined and can never turn into an error — input 101, 150 or 500 values, or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100 values followed by `"not-a-number"`, and the output is the same 100 lines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m An off-by-one in the bound is invisible on small inputs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `item_count_boundaries` (0, 1, 2, 98, 99, 100, 101, 102, 150, 500),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `garbage_after_hundred_items_is_never_read`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `matching_failure_at_every_boundary`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed with the mutation `while i < 99`: the suite fails on the 100-item case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Matching failure and EOF are the same branch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf` returns `0` on a matching failure and `EOF` (`-1`) on end of input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` checks `!= 1`, so both take the same `break` and the distinction never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m surfaces. `scan_i32` collapses both to `None`. Only the values read *before* the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bad token are transformed and printed — `"1 2 x 3 4"` prints two lines, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m four. A sign with no digits after it (`"-"`, `"+"`, `"- 5"`, `"--5"`) is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching failure, and `"12abc"` yields `12` and then fails on the next call.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `matching_failure_truncates_the_input`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sign_without_digits_is_a_matching_failure`, `eof_immediately_after_digits`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. `int i` is left holding the count, and `len == 0` is silent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i` is declared outside the loop, so after the loop it is the number of values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m successfully read, and that is what is passed as `len`. With `len == 0` both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loops in `driver` run zero times: **no output at all**, not a blank line, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `return 0`. The uninitialised tail of `data[100]` is never read back, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust zero-initialised array is not observable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `empty_input_prints_nothing`, `whitespace_only_is_eof`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Output format, exit status, and stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%d\n", ...)` — one value per line, decimal, no padding, no separator,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and a trailing newline on the final line too. The program writes nothing to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr and always returns `0`, including for every error path above: there is no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input that makes it exit non-zero. A test that only diffed stdout would pass a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust program that exited 1, so the suite asserts the exit status on every case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed with the mutation `std::process::exit(1)`: the suite fails on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty-input case with `C 0 vs Rust 256`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes enumerated from the C source
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every `if`, early exit and branch in `main` / `driver` / `fma_array`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - empty input; whitespace-only input (each of the six `isspace` bytes, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   10,000-byte run)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 1 value; 2 values; 98, 99, **100**, 101, 102, 150, 500 values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - separators: space, `\n`, `\t`, `\r\n`, `\v`, `\f`, mixed runs, none
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - with and without a trailing newline; leading whitespace before the first value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - matching failure at position 0, 1, 2, 50, 98, 99, 100, 101
